<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="2,3-trans-flavanols biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6029" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Flavonoids are highly diverse class of plant polyphenols (more than 9000 compounds have been identified so far |CITS: [16137727]|) that impart much of the color and flavor of fruits and vegetables. They exhibit a wide range of biological functions in higher plants such as UV protection, flower coloration, and antimicrobial activity |CITS: [11130659]|. Whewn ingested, flavonoids exhibit antioxidant and anticancer properties |CITS: [12224601]| |CITS: [18645020]|. Flavan-3-ols represent a classs of flavonoids and are the building blocks of condensed tannins. They have antioxidant properties and clinical studies have shown ant-icancer protective effects of the flavanols catechin, epicatechin, gallocatechin, and epigallocatechin |CITS: [14725861]|. It has been shown that epicatechin present in dark chocolate has the potential to promote cardiovascular health in humans. |FRAME: EC-1.17.1.3| ,converts leucoanthocyanidins to their corresponding 2,3-&lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-flavan-3-ols.</Description>
  <Authors>
